cartridge "SNO-CAT/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1984"

' SNO-CAT
' A ColecoVision game based on "Sno-Cat", COMPUTE!'s Gazette August 1984
' (Commodore 64 and VIC-20; BASIC with a small machine-language routine).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: you are a ranger driving a snowcat up the slope to the Mount
' Crumb ski lodge to rescue skiers. Trees come down the course at you; steer
' round them. The snowcat shifts gears by itself: second at the start,
' third after 10 seconds, fourth after 50 (and, new here, OVER DRIVE after
' 100). Hold the button to brake: the trees slow to a crawl, but the
' distance stops counting while the clock runs on. Cover the 400 units of
' DISTANCE TO GO and the lodge comes into view: you made it. Touch a tree
' and you crash. Score = (300 - seconds) x (distance covered / 2), plus a
' bonus of 500 + 100 x level when you reach the lodge.
'
' From the original (orig/sno-cat_64.bas and its 94-byte interrupt routine
' at 828, read by hand; run in jsc64): trees in fixed lanes, at most one
' tree a lane, a lane picked at random once a game pass and refilled when
' its tree has left the course; trees move down one pixel a frame per gear
' (brake 1, second 2, third 3, fourth 4), the snowcat moves sideways one
' pixel a frame and never up or down; gear changes at 10 and 50 seconds
' with SHIFTING GEARS shown; DISTANCE TO GO 400, one unit a game pass
' (measured in jsc64: 400 units in 91 seconds, about 14 frames a unit),
' none while braking; the score formula and the 500 for making it; the
' best score; YOU CRASHED / YOU MADE IT; the engine note rising with the
' gear; the lodge coming down at the end.
'
' Additions and liberties: the course scrolls (smooth, 1-5 pixels a frame
' with the trees) instead of a white screen with only the trees moving;
' trees slide in under the top edge and out under the bottom one (clipped
' sprite patterns), never popping in; 8 lanes 26 pixels apart on a 208
' pixel course (the C64 had 7 lanes 36 apart on 224 pixels: in both, no
' gap between two lanes is wide enough to park in); at most 3 trees side by
' side (4 sprites a line); difficulty 1-9 from the VIC-20 version (a new
' tree is tried every 26 - 2 x level frames, 16 at level 5 like the C64;
' levels 1-3 never put more than 2 trees side by side); OVER DRIVE (5 pixels
' a frame) after 100 seconds: the listing names it but never selects it;
' the time is counted in real seconds (the listing used TI$ as a number, so
' 1:00 counted as 100); the level bonus; no play-again question; the
' snowcat steers 2 pixels a frame instead of 1 (a human-paced test driver
' crashed within 10-40 seconds at 1 pixel a frame on the narrower lanes).
'
' Controls: either controller; the stick steers left and right; hold a
' side button to brake. On the title, up/down choose, left/right set the
' level, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, the title tune
' played once. `set sprite count` always given. Own xorshift random
' numbers. Nothing flashes.

